{%- liquid
  # The block takes EITHER a Model ID from the app's library or a pasted file
  # URL. The id wins when both are filled, since it's the more specific choice.
  #
  # A Model ID is resolved in two ways, in this order:
  #
  #  1. From the shop metafield the app mirrors its library into
  #     (app/lib/model-library.server.ts). Costs nothing at page-view time and
  #     keeps working if the app is down — so it stays the preferred path.
  #  2. Failing that, the raw id is handed to viewer-3d.js, which fetches it from
  #     the app's /api/model/<id>. One request per viewer, but it cannot be
  #     defeated by a metafield scope or a Liquid namespace syntax difference.
  #
  # (2) exists because (1) alone kept failing in the field with no visible cause:
  # writing the shop metafield needs a scope this app doesn't request, and reading
  # an app-reserved namespace back in Liquid depends on API-version behaviour.
  # Either way the merchant just saw "not found" for every id, with no recourse.
  # Correctness first: the id now always resolves, and the fast path is an
  # optimisation rather than the only mechanism.

  # Where the client resolves an id from, preferred first.
  #
  # proxy_base is a RELATIVE path on the shop's own domain. Shopify's app proxy
  # ([app_proxy] in shopify.app.toml) forwards it to whatever this app's current
  # URL is, so there is nothing to hardcode, nothing to keep in sync, and — being
  # same-origin — no CORS involved.
  #
  # app_origin is the absolute fallback for a shop where the proxy hasn't been
  # deployed yet, or where it points somewhere that doesn't have this model —
  # which is every `shopify app dev` session, since the proxy keeps pointing at
  # the production URL in shopify.app.toml while the models being edited live in
  # the dev database.
  #
  # It is read from the metafield the app republishes on every library sync
  # (APP_URL_KEY in app/lib/model-library-constants.ts) rather than hardcoded.
  # The literal below is only the last resort: it is what a hardcoded origin
  # always decayed into — a stale `shopify app dev` tunnel that had stopped
  # existing, so every fetch died with the browser's opaque "Failed to fetch"
  # and the block quietly fell back to default settings. Keep it pointing at
  # production, and let the metafield handle anything that moves.
  assign proxy_base = '/apps/viewer-3d'
  assign app_origin = shop.metafields["$app:viewer3d"].app_url.value
  if app_origin == blank
    assign app_origin = 'https://3d-viewer-app.bplugins.com'
  endif

  assign model_id = block.settings.model_id | strip
  assign model_url = ''
  assign model_config = ''
  assign id_unresolved = false

  if model_id != blank
    # The app-reserved namespace first, the merchant-owned one as a fallback.
    #
    # `custom` was the only source until it turned out this app has no scope
    # granting it write access to *shop*-owned metafields — so that metafield was
    # always empty and every id reported "not found". `$app:viewer3d` is reserved
    # to this app (Shopify expands it to app--<app-id>--viewer3d), needs no extra
    # scope, and is readable from this app's own extension. The legacy lookup
    # stays so a shop whose `custom` metafield did get populated keeps working.
    assign library = shop.metafields["$app:viewer3d"].library.value
    if library == blank
      assign library = shop.metafields.custom.viewer_3d_library.value
    endif
    assign entry = library[model_id]
    if entry
      assign model_url = entry.url
      assign model_config = entry.config
    else
      # No longer an error state. The id goes to the client, which resolves it
      # against the app — see the two-path note at the top. Previously this
      # rendered a dead "unknown model id" panel and the viewer never appeared.
      assign id_unresolved = true
    endif
  else
    assign model_url = block.settings.model_url
  endif

  # See product_viewer.liquid: a color literal is painted as CSS, anything else
  # (an image URL) is left to view3d, with white behind the canvas meanwhile.
  assign viewer_bg = block.settings.background_color
  assign viewer_bg_css = '#ffffff'
  assign viewer_bg_first_char = viewer_bg | slice: 0
  if viewer_bg_first_char == '#'
    assign viewer_bg_css = viewer_bg
  endif

  # A model referenced by id uses its own saved height/background, so the block's
  # appearance settings act as fallbacks rather than overrides.
  assign viewer_height = block.settings.height
  if model_config.height
    assign viewer_height = model_config.height
  endif
  if model_config.background
    assign viewer_bg = model_config.background
    assign viewer_bg_css = '#ffffff'
    assign viewer_bg_first_char = viewer_bg | slice: 0
    if viewer_bg_first_char == '#'
      assign viewer_bg_css = viewer_bg
    endif
  endif
-%}

{%- comment -%}
  The page block has no product metafield to read a saved config from, so its
  two interaction settings are projected into the same data-config JSON the
  product block emits. They used to ride on data-auto-rotate /
  data-show-controls attributes that viewer-3d.js never read at all — so
  "Auto rotate" and "Show on-screen controls" silently did nothing here, and
  this viewer couldn't help but behave differently from the admin preview.
  Everything not named here falls back to the app defaults in viewer-3d.js.

  "Show on-screen controls" now covers only zoom and fullscreen, which is the
  whole of the default control bar. It used to also drive the camera-reset button
  and the animation clip switcher; both are off by default and are opted into per
  model in the app, so projecting this block-level switch onto them would have
  quietly overridden that choice — and switching the clip selector on here would
  additionally have started an animation, since a visible switcher is what makes
  a model animate at all.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- liquid
  # `nil | json` emits a literal null, which viewer-3d.js would have to treat as
  # "use the default" rather than "off" — it does (see compact()), but don't rely
  # on that: resolve to a real boolean here. `allow_false: true` is essential,
  # otherwise Liquid's `default` filter would rewrite a deliberate false to true.
  assign auto_rotate = block.settings.auto_rotate | default: true, allow_false: true
  assign show_controls = block.settings.show_controls | default: true, allow_false: true
-%}
{%- comment -%}
  The model-source attributes, built here rather than with an `if` inline in the
  tag below.

  An inline `{%- if -%}` between two attributes strips the whitespace on both
  sides of itself, so `data-view3d` and the attribute after it were emitted as
  one run of characters: `data-view3ddata-model-id="…"`. That silently destroyed
  the `data-view3d` marker the script selects on, so the block rendered its
  canvas and nothing ever initialised it — a viewer-shaped empty box.

  Captured and interpolated as a single value, the surrounding spaces in the tag
  are literal and can't be stripped by a tag that isn't there.

  The Model ID is emitted whenever there is one, even when Liquid already found
  the URL — it used to be the `else` branch of the URL, which left a hole: a
  library entry carrying a url but no usable config (an older sync, or one that
  ran before the model's settings were last saved) set data-model-url, so the
  script had no reason to ask the app for anything, and the viewer rendered with
  the three block-level keys below and app defaults for everything else. Silent,
  and indistinguishable from "the merchant never turned those settings on".

  data-config-complete says which of those two cases this is, so the script can
  top up a partial config from the app instead of guessing.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- capture source_attrs -%}
{%- if model_url != blank -%}
data-model-url="{{ model_url | escape }}"
{%- endif -%}
{%- if model_id != blank %} data-model-id="{{ model_id | escape }}" data-proxy-base="{{ proxy_base | escape }}" data-api-base="{{ app_origin | escape }}"
{%- endif -%}
{%- if model_config != blank %} data-config-complete="1"
{%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}

{%- capture viewer_config -%}
{%- if model_config != blank -%}
{{ model_config | json }}
{%- else -%}
{
  "autoRotate": {{ auto_rotate | json }},
  "showZoomButtons": {{ show_controls | json }},
  "showFullscreenButton": {{ show_controls | json }}
}
{%- endif -%}
{%- endcapture -%}



<div class="viewer-3d-block" {{ block.shopify_attributes }}>
  {%- comment -%}
    One viewer element for both resolution paths. When Liquid resolved the id (or
    a URL was pasted) data-model-url is set and viewer-3d.js initialises straight
    away. Otherwise data-model-id + data-api-base are set instead and the script
    fetches the model before initialising. It is the same element either way, so
    nothing downstream — sizing, config merging, the toolbar — has to care which
    path was taken.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if model_url != blank or id_unresolved -%}
    <div
      id="viewer-3d-{{ block.id }}"
      class="viewer-3d"
      style="--viewer-height: {{ viewer_height }}px; --viewer-bg: {{ viewer_bg_css }}; background: {{ viewer_bg_css }};"
      data-view3d {{ source_attrs }}
      data-viewer-height="{{ viewer_height }}"
      data-viewer-bg="{{ viewer_bg }}"
      data-placement="page"
      {%- if model_id != blank %} data-track-target="model:{{ model_id | escape }}"{% endif %}
      {%- comment -%}
        Engagement counts for the Analytics page; never from the theme editor.
      {%- endcomment -%}
      {%- unless request.design_mode %} data-track-url="{{ proxy_base | escape }}/track"{% endunless %}
      {%- comment -%}
        Double-quoted, not single: Liquid's `escape` turns & < > " into entities
        but leaves ' alone, so an apostrophe in a config value (variant and
        iosSrc are free text) would terminate a single-quoted attribute early.
      {%- endcomment %}
      data-config="{{ viewer_config | escape }}"
    >
      <canvas class="view3d-canvas"></canvas>
    </div>

    {%- if block.settings.caption != blank -%}
      <p class="viewer-3d__caption">{{ block.settings.caption }}</p>
    {%- endif -%}
  {%- else -%}
    <div class="viewer-3d__empty">
      <p>{{ 'viewer_3d.no_model_page' | t }}</p>
    </div>
  {%- endif -%}
</div>

<style>
  #shopify-block-{{ block.id }} {
    --viewer-height: {{ viewer_height }}px;
    width: 100%;e\/
    --viewer-bg: {{ viewer_bg_css }};
  }
</style>

{%- comment -%}
  The vendor bundle (egjs-view3d + three.js, ~725KB) and its stylesheet are
  passed as URLs rather than loaded eagerly; viewer-3d.js fetches them on the
  first mount. See the same note in product_viewer.liquid for why.
{%- endcomment -%}
{{ 'viewer-3d.css' | asset_url | stylesheet_tag }}
<script
  src="{{ 'viewer-3d.js' | asset_url }}"
  data-vendor-js="{{ 'view3d.pkgd.min.js' | asset_url }}"
  data-vendor-css="{{ 'view3d-bundle.min.css' | asset_url }}"
  defer
></script>

{% schema %}
{
  "name": "3D Viewer — Any Model",
  "target": "section",
  "settings": [
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Model source"
    },
    {
      "type": "paragraph",
      "content": "Use either a Model ID or a file URL. The Model ID also applies that model's saved viewer settings, so it's the better option — copy it from the app under 3D Models → (your model) → Model ID."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "model_id",
      "label": "Model ID",
      "info": "From the app's 3D Models library. Takes priority over the URL below."
    },
    {
      "type": "url",
      "id": "model_url",
      "label": "Model URL (.glb / .gltf)",
      "info": "Alternative to a Model ID: paste a direct file URL from Settings → Files."
    },
    {
      "type": "text",
      "id": "caption",
      "label": "Caption (optional)"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Appearance"
    },
    {
      "type": "range",
      "id": "height",
      "label": "Viewer height",
      "min": 200,
      "max": 800,
      "step": 50,
      "default": 500,
      "unit": "px"
    },
    {
      "type": "color",
      "id": "background_color",
      "label": "Background color",
      "default": "#ffffff"
    },
    {
      "type": "header",
      "content": "Interaction"
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "auto_rotate",
      "label": "Auto rotate",
      "default": true
    },
    {
      "type": "checkbox",
      "id": "show_controls",
      "label": "Show on-screen controls",
      "default": true
    }
  ]
}
{% endschema %}
